## What the WorkOS plugin adds

- **`workos` skill**: Implementation + debugging help across **AuthKit (authentication)**, **SSO/SAML**, **Directory Sync/SCIM**, **RBAC/FGA**, **MFA**, **Vault**, **Audit Logs**, **Admin Portal**, **webhooks/events**, **custom domains**, and **migrations** (Auth0/Clerk/Cognito/Firebase/Supabase/Stytch/Descope/Better Auth). It’s best when you’re wiring backend SDK calls, callback handling, session strategy, and event/webhook correctness.
- **`workos-widgets` skill**: End-to-end setup for **WorkOS Widgets** (User Management, User Profile, Admin Portal SSO Connection, Domain Verification): detects your frontend framework patterns and shows the exact **“mint widget access token on backend → embed widget on frontend”** flow.

You can invoke them directly with **`/workos`** or **`/workos-widgets`** when you’re building or stuck.

## One concrete, useful thing you can do right now (Widgets token endpoint)

If you want a fast “this is valuable” win in almost any workspace: add a **single backend endpoint** that mints a WorkOS Widgets access token. That immediately unblocks embedding the User Management or User Profile widget in your app.

Here’s a minimal **Node/Express** example you can paste into your backend (adjust auth + user org mapping to your app):

```js
import express from "express";
import { WorkOS } from "@workos-inc/node";

const app = express();
const workos = new WorkOS(process.env.WORKOS_API_KEY);

// Example auth middleware placeholder
function requireUser(req, res, next) {
  // TODO: replace with your real session/JWT validation
  req.user = { id: "user_123", email: "dev@company.com", organizationId: "org_123" };
  next();
}

app.post("/api/workos/widgets/token", requireUser, async (req, res) => {
  try {
    // The key value: your app decides which org + user gets widget access.
    const { token } = await workos.widgets.getToken({
      userId: req.user.id,
      organizationId: req.user.organizationId,
      email: req.user.email,
    });

    res.json({ token });
  } catch (err) {
    res.status(500).json({ error: "Failed to mint WorkOS widget token" });
  }
});

app.listen(3001);
```

Once that exists, your frontend can fetch `/api/workos/widgets/token` and pass the token into the widget component.

If you tell me your stack (**Next.js / Remix / Express / FastAPI / Rails / etc.**) and which widget (**User Management** vs **User Profile**), I’ll tailor the exact endpoint + frontend embed to your framework using **`/workos-widgets`** so it’s drop-in for this workspace.

